      <article class="prose policy-prose">
        <h2 id="about-this-page">ABOUT THIS PAGE</h2>
        <p>This page sets out the policies and standards under which Trustora operates as an Employer of Record (EoR) partner. It is published so that clients, current and prospective employees, partners, and the public can understand how Trustora delivers its services, handles personal data, processes payroll, and governs its engagements.</p>
        <p>This policy establishes Trustora's operating standards. It is not a substitute for country-specific legal, tax, immigration, or employment advice, and it does not create rights beyond those provided by applicable law or by signed written engagement terms.</p>

        <h2 id="section-1">1. COMPANY OVERVIEW AND LEGAL IDENTITY</h2>
        <h3 id="section-1-1">1.1 Who We Are</h3>
        <p>TRUSTORA (SMC-PRIVATE) LIMITED is a company incorporated and established in the Islamic Republic of Pakistan. Trustora operates as an Employer of Record (EoR) partner: it provides employment administration and coordination, including Employer of Record and related specialist talent, payroll, people operations, and workplace services, through written engagement terms.</p>
        <h3 id="section-1-2">1.2 Service Reach and Entity Boundary</h3>
        <p>Trustora's service reach may involve clients, employees, and local partners in more than one country. Service reach does not mean that Trustora maintains a separate legal entity, employment license, or registration in every country in which services are delivered. Where Trustora delivers services outside Pakistan, it does so on the basis of written engagement terms and, where required, through qualified local partners, and it does not represent itself as a licensed employer or legal entity in any jurisdiction where it holds no such registration.</p>
        <h3 id="section-1-3">1.3 Legal Identity</h3>
        <p>The legal identity of Trustora is TRUSTORA (SMC-PRIVATE) LIMITED, established under the laws of Pakistan. Trustora maintains its statutory registrations, including its National Tax Number (NTN) and such other registrations as are required under applicable Pakistan law, and keeps current on its filing and compliance obligations.</p>
        <h3 id="section-1-4">1.4 This Policy Is Not Legal Advice</h3>
        <p>Nothing on this page constitutes legal, tax, immigration, or employment advice. Country-specific legal, tax, immigration, and employment requirements may require qualified local professionals. Trustora obtains and follows qualified advice where required before commencing or continuing an engagement.</p>

        <h2 id="section-2">2. PURPOSE, SCOPE, AND POLICY HIERARCHY</h2>
        <h3 id="section-2-1">2.1 Purpose</h3>
        <p>This policy defines the operating standards, responsibilities, and governance that Trustora follows when acting as an Employer of Record on behalf of its clients and their employees. It sets out Trustora's commitments to clients, to employees, and to the public.</p>
        <h3 id="section-2-2">2.2 Scope</h3>
        <p>These policies apply to:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>All Trustora employees and officers.</li>
          <li>All client employees for whom Trustora acts as Employer of Record.</li>
          <li>All contractors, consultants, and vendors engaged by Trustora.</li>
          <li>All client engagements, regardless of the employee's location.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 id="section-2-3">2.3 Policy Hierarchy</h3>
        <p>Where a specific country law, a client contract, or a local engagement agreement imposes a higher standard than this policy, the higher standard prevails. In case of conflict, applicable law and signed written engagement terms take precedence. Any conflict between this policy and an employee's offer letter is resolved in favour of the offer letter and applicable law.</p>
        <h3 id="section-2-4">2.4 Availability and Updates</h3>
        <p>This policy is published on trustora.net and is available to clients on request. It is reviewed at least annually, or sooner when required by law, business change, or an engagement need, and updates are published on this page.</p>

        <h2 id="section-3">3. EMPLOYMENT AND WORKPLACE STANDARDS</h2>
        <h3 id="section-3-1">3.1 Equal Employment Opportunity</h3>
        <p>Trustora is committed to providing equal opportunity in employment and does not discriminate against any employee or applicant on the basis of race, colour, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability, marital status, or any other status protected by applicable law. All employment decisions, including hiring, promotion, compensation, training, and termination, are based on merit, qualifications, and business need.</p>
        <h3 id="section-3-2">3.2 Harassment and Respect</h3>
        <p>Trustora maintains a workplace free from harassment, bullying, and retaliation. Harassment includes unwelcome conduct based on a protected characteristic that creates a hostile, intimidating, or offensive environment. Anyone who experiences or witnesses harassment may report it through the process set out in Section 18. Retaliation against anyone who reports in good faith is prohibited.</p>
        <h3 id="section-3-3">3.3 Code of Conduct and Ethics</h3>
        <p>Trustora and its people act with honesty, integrity, and professionalism in all dealings; comply with all applicable laws, regulations, and written engagement terms; avoid conflicts of interest; represent Trustora and its clients accurately; protect company and client resources; and respect the confidentiality of employee, client, and business information.</p>
        <h3 id="section-3-4">3.4 Conflict of Interest</h3>
        <p>Trustora's people avoid situations where personal, financial, or other interests could improperly influence their professional judgment or decisions. Actual, potential, or perceived conflicts of interest are disclosed in writing to management as soon as they arise.</p>
        <h3 id="section-3-5">3.5 Anti-Bribery and Anti-Corruption</h3>
        <p>Trustora prohibits bribery, kickbacks, and corrupt practices in all business dealings. No one acting on behalf of Trustora may offer, give, solicit, or accept anything of value to improperly influence a business decision. Gifts, hospitality, and entertainment must be reasonable, transparent, and in line with applicable law.</p>
        <h3 id="section-3-6">3.6 Sanctions and Integrity</h3>
        <p>Trustora does not knowingly do business with, employ, or make payments to any individual, entity, or jurisdiction subject to applicable economic or trade sanctions. Any concern that a client, candidate, or transaction may involve a sanctioned party is reported promptly, and the matter is not proceeded with while the concern remains unresolved.</p>
        <h3 id="section-3-7">3.7 Political Neutrality</h3>
        <p>Trustora does not make political contributions in the name of the Company and does not express political positions on behalf of clients or employees.</p>

        <h2 id="section-4">4. RECRUITMENT, SCREENING, AND BACKGROUND VERIFICATION</h2>
        <h3 id="section-4-1">4.1 Recruitment Standards</h3>
        <p>Trustora recruits and places talent on the basis of merit and the written requirements of the client. Job postings and role specifications accurately describe the role, its location, and its terms, and no false or misleading commitments are made to candidates.</p>
        <h3 id="section-4-2">4.2 Verification of Identity and Credentials</h3>
        <p>Before an offer of employment is extended, Trustora verifies a candidate's identity, work eligibility, qualifications, and references to the extent permitted by applicable law. Where a role requires a specific professional credential, proof of that credential is obtained and retained in the employee record.</p>
        <h3 id="section-4-3">4.3 Sanctions Screening</h3>
        <p>All candidates, clients, and counterparties are screened against applicable sanctions and restricted-party lists before an engagement begins and on a scheduled basis thereafter. Screening covers the individual, any entity they represent, and beneficial ownership information where available. Any match or near-match is escalated for review before any offer, contract, or payment is made, and no engagement proceeds while a screening question remains outstanding.</p>
        <h3 id="section-4-4">4.4 Candidate Data</h3>
        <p>Candidate data is collected only for the purpose of recruitment, is handled in line with Section 11, and is deleted when it is no longer required for a legitimate purpose or as required by applicable law.</p>

        <h2 id="section-5">5. OFFER LETTERS, CONTRACTS, AND COMPENSATION STRUCTURE</h2>
        <h3 id="section-5-1">5.1 Offer Letters</h3>
        <p>Every employment relationship is documented through a written offer letter and employment contract before active employment begins. Each offer letter states, as a minimum:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Job title and duties.</li>
          <li>Compensation, including the division into basic salary and allowances.</li>
          <li>Benefits provided and covered by Trustora on the employee's behalf.</li>
          <li>Work location and working hours.</li>
          <li>Probation period and notice requirements.</li>
          <li>Leave entitlements.</li>
          <li>Payment currency and payment schedule.</li>
          <li>Conditions precedent to employment, including successful screening.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 id="section-5-2">5.2 Compensation Structure</h3>
        <p>Compensation is defined as basic salary plus allowances, and the split between the two is stated explicitly in the employee's offer letter. Statutory deductions and contributions are computed on the basis of the structure set out in the offer letter and applicable law. Where the client defines the structure, that structure is reflected in the offer letter.</p>
        <h3 id="section-5-3">5.3 Everything in Writing</h3>
        <p>Nothing is promised to an employee that is not set out in writing. Benefits, deductions, and contributions covered for the employee are described in the offer letter. Changes to compensation, benefits, or other material terms are agreed in writing before they take effect.</p>
        <h3 id="section-5-4">5.4 Contracts and Documentation</h3>
        <p>Employment contracts state the terms required for the jurisdiction in which the employee works, including job title, duties, compensation, benefits, work location, hours, notice period, and termination terms. Contracts are completed and executed before active employment begins. Client engagement terms are governed by Section 16.</p>

        <h2 id="section-6">6. PAYROLL AND COMPENSATION ADMINISTRATION</h2>
        <h3 id="section-6-1">6.1 Payroll Cycle</h3>
        <p>Payroll is processed twice a month. Runs occur in the second week and the fourth week of each calendar month, on the second Friday and the fourth Friday of the month respectively. Where a scheduled pay date falls on a non-working day or public holiday, payroll is settled on the immediately preceding working day or as otherwise required by applicable law. A payroll calendar is maintained and communicated to employees and clients at the start of each calendar year.</p>
        <h3 id="section-6-2">6.2 Payment Currency</h3>
        <p>Employee salaries are decided and paid in the local currency of the employee's engagement. Salaries are denominated in local currency only; they are not tied to, nor varied with, foreign-currency amounts. Where a client engagement is denominated in a foreign currency, the salary is converted to the local currency at the point of offer and thereafter paid in local currency in accordance with the offer letter.</p>
        <h3 id="section-6-3">6.3 Cash-Flow Model</h3>
        <p>Payroll is funded on an invoice-based model. Trustora invoices the client for the gross payroll costs and service charges under the engagement terms, the client settles the invoice, and Trustora then runs payroll and pays employees from its own funds. Settlement of client invoices is tracked against the payroll calendar to ensure funds are received ahead of each payroll run.</p>
        <h3 id="section-6-4">6.4 Payroll Processing</h3>
        <p>Payroll computations cover salary, allowances, bonuses, deductions, statutory contributions, and taxes. Payroll is calculated accurately and delivered on time, with a clear record retained for both the employee and the client. Payroll data is reconciled before each run and any discrepancy is resolved before payment.</p>
        <h3 id="section-6-5">6.5 Payslips and Records</h3>
        <p>Each employee receives a complete payslip for every pay period showing gross pay, the split of basic salary and allowances, deductions, statutory contributions, and net pay. Payslips are provided to employees and payroll records are retained in line with statutory requirements and made available for audit.</p>
        <h3 id="section-6-6">6.6 Statutory Deductions and Contributions</h3>
        <p>Trustora withholds and remits all applicable statutory deductions and makes all applicable contributions, including income tax withholding, Employees' Old-Age Benefits Institution (EOBI) contributions, Provincial Employees' Social Security (SESSI) contributions, and gratuity or provident fund arrangements, in line with applicable law and the terms of the employee's offer letter. The specific items covered for each employee are those stated in the offer letter. Trustora maintains its statutory registrations and stays current on filing obligations.</p>
        <h3 id="section-6-7">6.7 Verification of Bank and Payment Details</h3>
        <p>Employee bank details are verified against official documentation before payments are released. Changes to payment details must be confirmed in writing and through verification controls to prevent fraud. Payments are made through approved banking channels, and any failed or rejected payment is investigated and resolved promptly.</p>
        <h3 id="section-6-8">6.8 Taxation and Compliance</h3>
        <p>Trustora withholds and remits applicable income tax in line with Pakistan law and the relevant engagement. Trustora maintains its NTN and other statutory registrations and stays current on filing obligations, including monthly, quarterly, and annual returns as required. Where services are delivered outside Pakistan, the applicable local obligations are addressed through the written engagement terms and qualified local advice.</p>

        <h2 id="section-7">7. BENEFITS</h2>
        <h3 id="section-7-1">7.1 Benefits Provided</h3>
        <p>Benefits provided to employees are those stated in the employee's offer letter. Benefits may include statutory benefits and any engagement-specific benefits agreed with the client. No benefit is implied by this policy that is not provided in writing.</p>
        <h3 id="section-7-2">7.2 Gratuity and Provident Fund</h3>
        <p>Gratuity and provident fund arrangements are governed by the terms of each engagement and the employee's offer letter. Where applicable law requires a gratuity or provident fund arrangement, Trustora complies with that requirement, and accruals and settlement are reconciled and paid in accordance with the engagement terms at the end of service.</p>
        <h3 id="section-7-3">7.3 Benefits Administration</h3>
        <p>Trustora administers benefits on behalf of the employee and the client in accordance with the written terms. Where a benefit is administered by a third party, Trustora verifies that the benefit is delivered and that records are maintained. Changes to benefits are agreed in writing before they take effect.</p>

        <h2 id="section-8">8. LEAVE AND ATTENDANCE</h2>
        <h3 id="section-8-1">8.1 Leave Entitlements</h3>
        <p>Leave entitlements, including annual leave, casual leave, sick leave, maternity and paternity leave, and public holidays, are granted in accordance with the statutory minimum applicable under the law of the employee's engagement and the written engagement terms. Where a client provides entitlements above the statutory minimum, the higher entitlement applies as stated in the offer letter.</p>
        <h3 id="section-8-2">8.2 Leave Process</h3>
        <p>Employees record absences according to the leave process applicable to their engagement. Leave is approved in advance wherever possible. Sick leave may be supported by appropriate documentation as required by law or the engagement terms. Leave records are maintained per employee and reconciled against payroll to ensure accurate deduction and accrual.</p>
        <h3 id="section-8-3">8.3 Unauthorized Absence</h3>
        <p>Unauthorized absence may be treated as a disciplinary matter in line with Section 19. Any absence is considered in light of the employee's specific circumstances and applicable law.</p>

        <h2 id="section-9">9. PROBATION, PERFORMANCE, AND DEVELOPMENT</h2>
        <h3 id="section-9-1">9.1 Probation Period</h3>
        <p>The default probation period is six months. During probation, the employee is assessed against the objectives of the role, and either party may end the engagement in line with the notice terms stated in the offer letter and applicable law. Probation periods may be adjusted in writing where the offer letter, client contract, or applicable law provides otherwise.</p>
        <h3 id="section-9-2">9.2 Performance Management</h3>
        <p>Employee performance is managed through client-defined objectives and regular reviews. Trustora supports the client in tracking progress, recording outcomes, and applying consistent performance standards. Performance expectations are communicated clearly at the start of the engagement and reviewed on a schedule agreed with the client.</p>
        <h3 id="section-9-3">9.3 Performance Improvement</h3>
        <p>Where performance does not meet expectations, the employee is given clear written feedback, a defined improvement period, and reasonable support. Disciplinary and performance action requires clear documentation and adherence to applicable law and the engagement terms</p>
        <h3 id="section-9-4">9.4 Development and Training</h3>
        <p>Trustora supports the professional development of employees within the limits of the engagement terms and budget. Training that is mandatory for legal or compliance reasons is provided by Trustora or the client as agreed in writing</p>

        <h2 id="section-10">10. TERMINATION, OFFBOARDING, AND SEVERANCE</h2>
        <h3 id="section-10-1">10.1 Grounds for Termination</h3>
        <p>Employment may end by resignation, completion of the engagement, expiry of a fixed-term contract, or dismissal for cause, in each case in accordance with the offer letter, the written engagement terms, and applicable law. Termination must not be discriminatory or retaliatory.</p>
        <h3 id="section-10-2">10.2 Notice and Severance</h3>
        <p>Termination follows the contractual notice period and, where applicable, statutory minimum notice and severance entitlements. Severance is provided at the statutory minimum unless the engagement terms provide more favourable terms, in which case the more favourable terms apply.</p>
        <h3 id="section-10-3">10.3 Offboarding Process</h3>
        <p>On termination, Trustora manages:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Final pay and settlement of entitlements.</li>
          <li>Return of company and client assets and credentials.</li>
          <li>Revocation of access to systems and data.</li>
          <li>Exit documentation, statutory notifications, and compliance filings.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Final settlement is computed and reviewed before payment, and access revocation is verified against the access register.</p>
        <h3 id="section-10-4">10.4 Exit Feedback</h3>
        <p>Where practical, an exit discussion is held to capture feedback and to ensure that offboarding is complete. Feedback is used to improve Trustora's processes and is handled in line with Section 13.</p>

        <h2 id="section-11">11. DATA PROTECTION AND PRIVACY</h2>
        <h3 id="section-11-1">11.1 Data Protection Framework</h3>
        <p>Trustora handles personal data lawfully, fairly, and transparently, in line with the principles of the Pakistan Personal Data Protection Act and GDPR-aligned principles for its international clients. Where an engagement is subject to a stricter data-protection regime, the stricter regime applies as set out in the engagement terms.</p>
        <h3 id="section-11-2">11.2 Data Protection Principles</h3>
        <p>Personal data is:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Collected for specific, legitimate purposes.</li>
          <li>Limited to what is necessary.</li>
          <li>Accurate and kept up to date.</li>
          <li>Kept only as long as required.</li>
          <li>Secured against unauthorized access, loss, or misuse.</li>
          <li>Processed on a documented lawful basis.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 id="section-11-3">11.3 Transparency</h3>
        <p>Trustora documents the lawful basis for each processing activity and provides clear privacy information to employees and candidates. Individuals are informed of what data is collected, why it is collected, how long it is kept, and the rights available to them.</p>
        <h3 id="section-11-4">11.4 Rights of Individuals</h3>
        <p>Trustora supports the exercise of data-subject rights, including access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability, and objection, within the timeframes and limits set by applicable law. Requests are logged, assigned, and completed within the statutory timeframe.</p>
        <h3 id="section-11-5">11.5 Confidentiality</h3>
        <p>All employee, client, and business data is confidential. Access to personal and company data is limited to those who require it for their role. Sharing, copying, or exposing confidential information without authorization is a serious matter and may result in disciplinary action, including termination.</p>
        <h3 id="section-11-6">11.6 Access Controls and Security</h3>
        <p>Access to systems, payroll platforms, and data repositories is role-based and granted on a need-to-know basis. Strong passwords, multi-factor authentication, and approved software are used, and access is reviewed on a scheduled basis. Access to confidential information, client data, research data, and AI-generated content is governed by Trustora's security standards.</p>
        <h3 id="section-11-7">11.7 Cross-Border Data Transfers</h3>
        <p>Where personal data is transferred outside Pakistan, Trustora ensures that the transfer is supported by an appropriate lawful mechanism and that the destination provides adequate protection. Cross-border transfers are documented in the data-processing terms of the relevant engagement.</p>
        <h3 id="section-11-8">11.8 AI-Generated Content</h3>
        <p>Any AI-generated content used in official documents, proposals, or communications is reviewed, verified, and refined before use. AI tools are used only within the limits of this policy and applicable law. Personal data is not entered into AI tools that are not approved, and AI output is not relied upon for compliance decisions without human verification.</p>
        <h3 id="section-11-9">11.9 Data Retention and Disposal</h3>
        <p>Personal and payroll data is retained only for the period required by law and business need, then securely destroyed. Records disposal follows documented procedures and is auditable. Retention periods are applied according to the legal requirement for each record type.</p>

        <h2 id="section-12">12. CYBERSECURITY AND INFORMATION SECURITY</h2>
        <h3 id="section-12-1">12.1 Security Baseline</h3>
        <p>Trustora maintains a security baseline covering accounts, devices, networks, and data. Multi-factor authentication is required on all accounts and systems that support it. Only approved systems and software are used, and security controls are not circumvented.</p>
        <h3 id="section-12-2">12.2 Passwords and Accounts</h3>
        <p>Passwords must be strong and unique to Trustora systems. Shared accounts are prohibited. Credentials are never disclosed, and suspected compromise is reported immediately.</p>
        <h3 id="section-12-3">12.3 Devices and Remote Access</h3>
        <p>Company-issued devices are configured to the approved standard. Remote access to Trustora systems is made only through approved, secure methods. Devices that are lost or stolen are reported immediately so that access can be revoked and data protected.</p>
        <h3 id="section-12-4">12.4 Data Handling</h3>
        <p>Sensitive information, including proposal documentation, customer information, research data, and organizational knowledge, remains confidential and protected at all times, including when working remotely. File sharing of confidential data is restricted to approved repositories, and AI-generated content is governed by Section 11.8.</p>
        <h3 id="section-12-5">12.5 Reporting</h3>
        <p>Security incidents, suspected breaches, and unusual activity are reported immediately through the incident process in Section 14.</p>

        <h2 id="section-13">13. HEALTH, SAFETY, AND WELL-BEING</h2>
        <h3 id="section-13-1">13.1 Safe Work Environment</h3>
        <p>Trustora promotes a safe, healthy, and inclusive work environment for all employees, including remote workers. Remote employees are expected to maintain a professional, safe, and secure home work environment.</p>
        <h3 id="section-13-2">13.2 Incident Reporting</h3>
        <p>Accidents, injuries, safety concerns, and near-misses are reported as soon as possible. Trustora complies with applicable health, safety, and welfare requirements in each jurisdiction where services are delivered.</p>
        <h3 id="section-13-3">13.3 Well-Being Support</h3>
        <p>Trustora encourages a culture in which employees can raise well-being concerns without stigma. Where engagement terms provide an employee assistance or well-being program, employees are informed of it during onboarding.</p>

        <h2 id="section-14">14. INCIDENT RESPONSE AND DATA BREACH MANAGEMENT</h2>
        <h3 id="section-14-1">14.1 Incident Definition</h3>
        <p>An incident is any event that may compromise the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of Trustora systems or of the personal or confidential data Trustora handles, including data loss, unauthorized access, ransomware, phishing compromise, and accidental disclosure.</p>
        <h3 id="section-14-2">14.2 Incident Response</h3>
        <p>Incidents are contained, investigated, and resolved promptly. Roles and responsibilities during an incident are defined in advance, and an incident log records the detection, containment, investigation, and resolution steps. The initial response aims to contain the incident, preserve evidence, and assess its scope and impact.</p>
        <h3 id="section-14-3">14.3 Escalation and Notification</h3>
        <p>Internal escalation occurs within 24 hours of detection. External notification is made where required by applicable law or the engagement terms, within the timeframe those requirements set (including any GDPR-aligned 72-hour obligation where applicable). Affected clients are notified in line with their engagement terms.</p>
        <h3 id="section-14-4">14.4 Notification to Individuals</h3>
        <p>Where an incident is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of individuals, those individuals are notified without undue delay in line with applicable law. Notification describes the nature of the incident and the measures taken to address it.</p>
        <h3 id="section-14-5">14.5 Review and Learning</h3>
        <p>After an incident is resolved, a review is conducted to identify root causes and prevent recurrence. Remediation actions are tracked to completion.</p>

        <h2 id="section-15">15. REMOTE WORK AND WORKPLACE STANDARDS</h2>
        <h3 id="section-15-1">15.1 Remote Work</h3>
        <p>Fully remote roles require excellent communication, professionalism, reliability, and the ability to manage multiple priorities. Remote work arrangements are documented in the offer letter or engagement terms.</p>
        <h3 id="section-15-2">15.2 Remote Working Standards</h3>
        <p>Organizational cybersecurity policies apply at all times and only approved systems and software are used. Company and client systems are not used for unrelated or personal activity in a way that creates risk. Working hours and availability are set out in the offer letter or engagement terms.</p>
        <h3 id="section-15-3">15.3 Confidentiality When Remote</h3>
        <p>Sensitive information remains confidential and protected at all times, including when working remotely. Reasonable steps are taken to protect screens and materials from view by others, and confidential matters are not discussed in public spaces.</p>
        <h3 id="section-15-4">15.4 Communication and Availability</h3>
        <p>Employees respond to work communications within the response times agreed with the client, attend scheduled meetings, and manage dependencies clearly. Persistent failure to meet availability expectations may be handled under Section 19.</p>

        <h2 id="section-16">16. CLIENT ENGAGEMENT AND SERVICE DELIVERY</h2>
        <h3 id="section-16-1">16.1 Engagement Lifecycle</h3>
        <p>Client engagements follow a defined lifecycle: screening and qualification, written contracting, onboarding, service delivery, and exit. Each stage is governed by this section.</p>
        <h3 id="section-16-2">16.2 Client Screening and Qualification</h3>
        <p>Prospective clients are screened for legitimacy, financial standing, and sanctions status before an engagement begins. Sanctions screening is performed in line with Section 4.3. An engagement does not proceed where a screening question remains outstanding or where the engagement would create an integrity risk for Trustora.</p>
        <h3 id="section-16-3">16.3 Written Engagement Terms</h3>
        <p>Every client engagement is documented in a written agreement covering scope of services, pricing, payment terms, delivery responsibilities, confidentiality, data protection, liability, and what each party may state publicly. No engagement begins without an executed written agreement.</p>
        <h3 id="section-16-4">16.4 Scope of Services</h3>
        <p>Trustora acts within the defined scope of the engagement and does not perform services outside it. Any change to scope is agreed in writing before work proceeds, including any adjustment to price or timelines.</p>
        <h3 id="section-16-5">16.5 Service Boundaries</h3>
        <p>Trustora does not represent itself as a licensed employer or legal entity in jurisdictions where it holds no registration. The service-vs-entity boundary is clearly communicated to clients and is stated in engagement terms. Where a client expects a service that requires a local license Trustora does not hold, Trustora says so clearly and, where appropriate, directs the client to a qualified local provider.</p>
        <h3 id="section-16-6">16.6 Quality Standards</h3>
        <p>Trustora maintains documented quality standards across onboarding, payroll, compliance, and reporting. Work is delivered accurately, on time, and in accordance with the engagement terms, with clear record keeping.</p>
        <h3 id="section-16-7">16.7 Payroll Reporting</h3>
        <p>Payslips and payroll records are provided to employees. Reports to clients are provided as the engagement terms provide; where the engagement terms do not provide for employee-level reporting to the client, employee pay data is not shared with the client. Aggregate reporting, where agreed, is provided in line with the engagement terms.</p>
        <h3 id="section-16-8">16.8 Communications and Public Representations</h3>
        <p>No one may publicly represent Trustora's legal identity, licenses, countries of operation, or client outcomes unless the facts have been approved for publication. Claims follow approved evidence. Client names, logos, metrics, quotes, and case studies are published only with the client's written approval.</p>
        <h3 id="section-16-9">16.9 Client Exit and Transition</h3>
        <p>On termination of an engagement, Trustora completes final payroll and settlements, returns client data in line with the engagement terms, revokes access, and provides transition support as agreed.</p>
        <h3 id="section-16-10">16.10 Subcontracting</h3>
        <p>Trustora does not subcontract client engagements to other employers of record or intermediaries without the client's prior written consent. Where local partners are required to deliver services in a jurisdiction, their role is disclosed in the engagement terms.</p>

        <h2 id="section-17">17. RECORD KEEPING AND DOCUMENTATION</h2>
        <h3 id="section-17-1">17.1 Record Keeping Standards</h3>
        <p>Trustora maintains organized documentation, consistent file structures, version control, meeting notes, and records of payroll, compliance, and engagement activities. Documentation is maintained in secure repositories with role-based access.</p>
        <h3 id="section-17-2">17.2 Retention</h3>
        <p>Records are retained for the period required by applicable law for each record type, then securely destroyed. Contracts, payroll, and statutory records are retained per the legal requirement applicable to each. Disposal is logged and auditable.</p>
        <h3 id="section-17-3">17.3 Confidentiality of Records</h3>
        <p>Records are accessible only to people with a legitimate need. Records are not removed from approved repositories without authorization.</p>

        <h2 id="section-18">18. COMPLAINTS, GRIEVANCES, AND WHISTLEBLOWING</h2>
        <h3 id="section-18-1">18.1 Raising a Concern</h3>
        <p>Any employee or client who has a concern about this policy, compliance, or conduct may raise it confidentially with management without fear of retaliation. Concerns may be raised in person, in writing, or through any channel published by Trustora from time to time.</p>
        <h3 id="section-18-2">18.2 Grievance Process</h3>
        <p>Grievances are acknowledged, investigated fairly and promptly, and resolved with appropriate action, consistent with applicable law and the engagement terms. The complainant is informed of the outcome to the extent permitted by confidentiality obligations.</p>
        <h3 id="section-18-3">18.3 Whistleblowing</h3>
        <p>Employees who in good faith report suspected misconduct, fraud, corruption, or unlawful conduct are protected from retaliation. Reports are treated as confidential and investigated by the appropriate person.</p>
        <h3 id="section-18-4">18.4 Non-Retaliation</h3>
        <p>Retaliation against anyone who reports a concern in good faith is strictly prohibited and is itself a disciplinary matter under Section 19.</p>

        <h2 id="section-19">19. DISCIPLINE AND CONSEQUENCES</h2>
        <h3 id="section-19-1">19.1 Standards of Conduct</h3>
        <p>Non-compliance with this policy, applicable law, or written engagement terms may result in corrective action consistent with local legal requirements, up to and including termination and, where appropriate, legal action.</p>
        <h3 id="section-19-2">19.2 Fair Process</h3>
        <p>Disciplinary action follows a fair process: the person concerned is informed of the allegation, given an opportunity to respond, and a decision is made on the evidence.</p>
        <h3 id="section-19-3">19.3 Serious Misconduct</h3>
        <p>Serious misconduct, including fraud, bribery, data theft, harassment, or deliberate misrepresentation, may result in summary dismissal where permitted by applicable law.</p>

        <h2 id="section-20">20. POLICY GOVERNANCE AND REVIEW</h2>
        <h3 id="section-20-1">20.1 Ownership</h3>
        <p>This policy is owned by Trustora management.</p>
        <h3 id="section-20-2">20.2 Review Cycle</h3>
        <p>This policy is reviewed at least annually, or sooner when required by law, business change, or an engagement need. Updates are published on this page and reflected in the version history below.</p>
        <h3 id="section-20-3">20.3 Version History</h3>
        <p>Version 2.0 (public website edition) - [date]. This edition re-issued as the public policy page on trustora.net.</p>
        <p>Version 1.0 - [date]. Initial publication.</p>
        <h3 id="section-20-4">20.4 Questions</h3>
        <p>Questions about this policy, or about Trustora's handling of personal data, may be directed to Trustora through the contact channels on <a href="/contact/">trustora.net</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="section-21">21. GENERAL PROVISIONS</h2>
        <h3 id="section-21-1">21.1 Severability</h3>
        <p>If any provision of this policy is held to be invalid or unenforceable, the remaining provisions continue in force.</p>
        <h3 id="section-21-2">21.2 Waiver</h3>
        <p>A failure to enforce a provision on one occasion is not a waiver of the provision on another occasion.</p>
        <h3 id="section-21-3">21.3 Governing Law</h3>
        <p>This policy is governed by the laws of the Islamic Republic of Pakistan, subject to the terms of each applicable contract and the law applicable to each engagement.</p>

        <h2 id="appendix-a">APPENDIX A - DEFINED TERMS</h2>
        <dl class="policy-definitions">
          <div>
            <dt>Employer of Record (EoR):</dt>
            <dd>an arrangement under which a company employs people on behalf of a client and takes on the legal employer responsibilities in the employee's jurisdiction.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Engagement terms:</dt>
            <dd>the written agreement between Trustora and a client covering the services to be delivered.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Offer letter:</dt>
            <dd>the written offer of employment to a candidate setting out the terms of employment, including the compensation structure.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Client:</dt>
            <dd>the party contracting with Trustora for EoR and related services.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Employee:</dt>
            <dd>a person employed by Trustora as an EoR on behalf of a client.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Payslip:</dt>
            <dd>the statement of gross pay, deductions, and net pay provided to an employee for each pay period.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Sanctions:</dt>
            <dd>economic or trade restrictions imposed by applicable authorities.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <p class="policy-end">END OF POLICY - (PUBLIC WEBSITE EDITION)</p>
      </article>
